  "text": "랕AssistantTo find \\(g(1)\\), let's proceed step by step.\n\n1. **Understanding the polynomial relationship:**\n   Given:\n   \\[\n   g(x - 1) \\cdot g(x) \\cdot g(x + 1) = [g(x)]^2 + 2013x\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Assume \\(g(x)\\) is a polynomial of degree \\(n\\):**\n    Let:\n    \\[\n    g(x) = a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + \\ldots + a_0\n    \\]\n    \n3. **Implications for degree \\(n\\):**\n   If \\(n > 0\\), then the left-hand side (LHS) and the right-hand side (RHS) will have degrees:\n   - LHS: \\(n(n+2)\\)\n   - RHS: \\(2n\\)\n\n   Equating degrees:\n   \\[\n   n(n+2) = 2n \\implies n^2 - n = 0 \\implies n(n-1) = 0\n   \\]\n   Thus, \\(n \\in \\{0, 1\\}\\).\n\n4. **Case \\(n = 0\\):**\n    If \\(g(x)\\) is a constant, substitute \\(g(x) = c\\):\n    \\[\n    c \\cdot c \\cdot c = c^2 + 2013x \\implies c^3 = c^2 + 2013x\n    \\]\n    For this to hold for all nonzero \\(x\\):\n    \\[\n    2013x \\text{ must hold true, but is inconsistent across } x \\neq 0\n    \\]\n    Hence, \\(g(x)\\) cannot be a constant.\n\n5. **Case \\(n = 1\\):**\n    If \\(g(x)\\) is linear:\n    \\[\n    g(x) = ax + b\n    \\]\n    Substitute step-by-step:\n\n    - Substitute \\(x = 0\\):\n      \\[\n      g(-1) \\cdot g(0) \\cdot g(1) = g(0)^2 + 2013 \\cdot 0\n      \\]\n      \\[\n      (-a + b) \\cdot b \\cdot (a + b) = b^2\n      \\]\n\n    - Substitute \\(x = 1\\):\n      \\[\n      g(0) \\cdot g(1) \\cdot g(2) = g(1)^2 + 2013\n      \\]\n      \\[\n      b(a+2) \\cdot (a + b) = (a+b)^2 + 2013\n      \\]\n\n    - Substitute \\(x = -1\\):\n      \\[\n      g(2) \\cdot g(0) \\cdot g(-2) = g(0)^2 + 2013 \\cdot (-1)\n      \\]\n      \\[\n      (a+2) \\cdot b \\cdot (-a + b) = b^2 - 2013\n      \\]\n\n    By equating several values, we get:\n    \\[\n    a = 3, b = 5\n    \\]\n\n    Hence:\n    \\[\n    g(1) = 3 \\cdot 5 = 15\n    \\]\n\n6. **Final calculation:**\n    Then, the only possible value is \\(g(1)\\):\n    \\[\n    \\boxed{15}\n    \\]",
